# Searching by file size
Size units in find
c = bytes, k = kilobytes (1024), M = megabytes, G = gigabytes
+ means greater than, - means less than, no prefix means EXACTLY

find /var -size +100M
Find files LARGER than 100MB. + = greater than. Critical for finding disk space hogs! Replace 100M with any size.

find /var/log -size -1k
Find files SMALLER than 1KB. - = less than. Find empty or tiny log files that may indicate issues.

find /tmp -size 0
Find EMPTY files (exactly 0 bytes). No + or - = exact match. Clean up empty files: find /tmp -size 0 -delete

find /var -size +10M -size -500M
Find files BETWEEN 10MB and 500MB. Combine -size options — implicit AND! Find medium-large files.

find / -type f -size +100M -exec ls -lh {} \; 2>/dev/null | sort -k5 -rh
Find all files >100MB with sizes, sorted largest first. Best way to find what's eating disk space!


# Searching by time — atime, mtime, ctime
-mtime = modification time (content changed)
-atime = access time (file read)
-ctime = change time (permissions/ownership changed)

N = exactly N days ago, +N = more than N days ago, -N = less than N days ago
-mmin, -amin, -cmin = same but in MINUTES (more precise!)

find /var/log -mtime +30
Files NOT modified in last 30 days. +30 = older than 30 days. Perfect for log cleanup! Safe to archive or delete.

find /opt/myapp -mtime -1
Files modified in last 24 hours. -1 = less than 1 day old. Find recently changed files — useful after deployments!

find /tmp -mmin -60
Files modified in last 60 MINUTES. -mmin = modification time in minutes. More precise than -mtime (days)!

find /etc -newer /etc/nginx/nginx.conf
Find files NEWER than reference file. Relative time comparison — files changed after nginx.conf was modified. Useful after config changes!

find /var/log -mtime 0
Files modified TODAY (within last 24h). mtime=0 = less than 1 day ago. Quick check of today's changed files!
